Date: Sun, 2 May 1993 01:53:14 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Global warming markets

I'd use futures/hedging reasoning to estimate the risks and
costs of global warming and alternative energy investments, even in 
the absence of actual markets.  In turn this reasoning might be used to 
design such markets.

At one extreme we have a planet using non-fossil
fuels, conservation, etc. where CO2 levels are too low to cause
significant damage.  At the other extreme we have a planet where fossil
fuels are used without regard to CO2 levels, some (uncertain, debatable) 
increases in CO2 and thereby damages due to increased weather variability 
and sea level rise.  We have a spectrum in between; investing extra money 
to build non-fossil instead of fossil means some decrease in future 
damages, as long as transaction costs are low and energy investment
in general is not subsidized.

Let's define Switch as the NPV of negative cash flows from the extra
cost of non-fossil, and Damage as the NPV of future damages.
Where dSwitch/dDamage < 1 it makes sense to switch, or invest in
non-fossil plants.  (ie it would make sense to a company if it owned
the entire planet, or a market existed that perfectly distributed
utility between non-fossil investors and damage victims).

The damage in a given year will be some (uncertain, debatable) function 
f weather variability and sea level rise, which in turn is some
(uncertain, debatable) function of CO2 levels.

By the Coase theorem, some arbitrary combination of seaside property
owners, insurance ratepayers, and CO2 emitters can pay for the 
cost of switching and/or damages, as long as there is a market
for reallocating these funds to the optimal solution (rebuilding
or abandoning damaged real estate, conservation, non-fossil energy
production, etc.)  This market assumes we can reasonably measure
CO2 emissions and reasonably discriminate additional hurricane,
real estate, etc. damages caused by CO2 after they have happened.
How can such a market be implemented with low transaction costs?

Futures markets can be used to estimate damage cash flows well
in advance, for comparison to the extra cost of non-fossil plant.
Idea futures might be used to gain a market consensus of future CO2
levels, and also consensus functions relating CO2 levels to weather
variability and sea level rise.  Richard Sandor's catastrophe futures 
(based on future payouts on catastrophe insurance, eg for hurricanes, 
flooding, etc.) can then be used to estimate specific damages.

To complicate matters further, there may be future increases in
agricultural and timber productivity due to enchanced plant growth
at higher CO2 levels.  Regions of agricultural productivity may
shift, creating new winners and losers.  This is another major area of 
uncertainty and debate.  Measuring these impacts, and especially
discriminating cause & effect to CO2, may be too difficult in these
areas.

Fears of global warming have already impacted the market directly, increasing 
the cost of hurricane insurance due to the theory that global warming 
increases weather variability.  There has also been major indirect impact
through regulation and government mandated spending on conservation, 
non-fossil energy sources, etc.  Even if we could create "regulation
futures" markets, based on the estimated costs of regulation in future
years (whose estimate?), it seems intractable to discriminate the
impact of CO2 levels on these regulations via global warming concerns 
from the many other causes of government regulation.

