Date: Sun, 2 May 93 22:07:35 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: Rational (racial) Discrimination

"Maybe I missed something but wouldn't stopping Deans friends only have a
rational basis if _all_ white people were similarly harassed in all black
neighborhoods? ... Wouldn't the rational basis be that someone who did not
"fit" the "normal" population of an area "must", for some reason, be there
for criminal activity. " (Matt)

I don't think so. To begin with, the issue is not "must" but probability.
If the odds that someone is about to commit a crime are, say, 40%, it is
reasonable to impose small costs on him (searching him to see if he has a
gun) in order to avoid large costs to the victim. Different legal
institutions might handle this in different ways, with or without
compensation to the innocent suspect, but no reasonable system will refuse
to take any action until the probability of guilt is 100%.

Further, the rational basis in the example depends on the specific facts of
the example--a low income black area with a very high crime rate adjacent
to a middle class white area. Given those facts, it might be reasonable to
think that a black in the white area was more likely to be there for
criminal purposes than a random white in the white area. How reasonable it
was would depend on more information--how many blacks lived or worked in
the white area, how much crime was committed by people from the one area
against victims in the other, etc. The same argument would apply to a white
in a black area only if you had a low income white area with a very high
crime rate adjacent to a middle class black area, which is possible but a
lot less common.

Of course, one could imagine other circumstances in which it would be
rational to suspect a white in a black area--for example, a situation with
a lot of racial hostility, where whites had been going into an adjacent
black area to beat up blacks. The basic point is that someone's race is an
easily observed fact which, under some circumstances, is evidence relevant
to the probability that he is doing something illegal. Police will and
should use such evidence, like  other evidence, in deciding how to act--who
to search, for example. This is a separate issue from the question of what
rights police ought to have--whether they ought to be able to search
anyone, for example, or how high a probability of guilt should be required,
or whether they should have to compensate the victims of searches that do
not find anything, or ...  .

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


