Subject: RAND: Warning: Objectivism may be hazardous to your health 

Perry asked:

  >   Could someone say precisely what is wrong with objectivism, other than
  >   the crappy epsistemology? 

[ I suggested that Perry is using a private definition of objectivism,
  citing those aspects of Objectivism he rejects.  ]

Perry replies:

  For the most part, she doesn't even get in to the worst aspects of her
  philosophy in "Atlas" or "The Fountainhead", just in her non-fiction.
  Most of her harangues in "Atlas" are fundamentally right, other than
  the obsessive "A is A" stuff, which isn't even the bulk of Galt's
  speech.

  As for "not what most people who read Rand call Objectivism", I must
  disagree there, too. For many of the people I know who've read Rand,
  she is the first introduction they get to any of these ideas at all.
  Also for most of those people, epistemology isn't even something they
  care about -- its the support of Capitalism and rational egoism that
  they get out of her, and that they like.

You are exactly right on both counts, and these are the reasons why
I said your use of the term was a private usage, at least in the
context of this list. 

The readers of Rand I had in mind are those who
read Objectivist philosophy, some of which I believe you are addressing
in your original question. Your correction helps to illustrate
my point. 

Most people who read Rand read her novels and think
that Objectivism means rational egoism and Capitalism. So did I,
when I first read Rand's novels. The implicit philosophy in Rand's
novels is not the same as the explicit philosophy is not the
same as final Objectivist philosophy. Her philosophy changed over time,
and the philosophy she expresses as an artist
was better than the philosophy she produced as a philosopher.

In the company of people who have read a few Rand novels, but have
no other exposure to Rand's philosophy, except knowing it is labelled
Objectivism, "objectivist" is probably the best label you can use, since they
have no better labels to use. For those who have read Rand's philosophy,
and perhaps other egoists, you might simply call yourself a rational egoist,
given your numerous fundamental disagreements with Objectivist philosophy.

I'm not out to save the purity of the label, I'm just suggesting
why you're having a failure to communicate about what is wrong with 
objectivism. I doubt that many people here have problems with capitalism
and rational egoism. 

