From: Stanton McCandlish <anton@hydra.unm.edu>
Subject: metaphysics vs epistemology

Stephen J. Whitrow

> In the vast majority of cases, the moral relativists are clearly right,
> and if an action is described as "immoral" then this just demonstrates a
> cultural bias, or the views of a particular bunch of statists. E.g., the
> idea that it is "wrong" to take certain drugs, or for private citizens
> to own specific weapons, or to practise cannibalism.
> 
> But what about the instance of randomly directed violence -- say, lethal
> violence -- initiated against a totally innocent third party? This might be
> carried out by private individuals, terrorists, or agents of a totalitarian
> regime (last but by no means least!). Is it morally right to have innocent
> sentient lifeforms, human beings for instance, tortured and murdered simply
> because they happened to be where they were, or because of the circumstances
> of their birth?

I enjoyed the rest of this semi-essay, but I would like to propose an idea 
that I have held firmly for many years.  From my own observations, it seems
to me that what people lump together under the term "morals" are actually
2 very different things.  For convenience, I choose to call them morals
and ethics, though my definition of these terms may not mesh with the con-
ventional ones.  To put it as simply as possible, I find that there are
social mores and behaviour patterns that are totally subjective (in the
sociological sense, not hard-science sense), such as: whether oral sex is
ungodly, being a democrat good, having a strong work ethic and family
values good, infringing "patents" on mathematical algorithms bad, etc etc.

On the other hand I see an entirely different set of "rules" that at least on
the surface seem to be universal or nearly so.  These I term ethics for lack of 
a better word.  Some examples of this, and most of them seem negatives by
the way: don't injure or kill people without provocation, don't steal from
people who are not your enemies, do not have sex with blood relations, do 
not rape your neighbor, etc etc.  

I have discussed this at length with several people, and some have disagreed.
But it seems to me that there are very clear cut distinctions between what
I am calling ethics and morals.  Morals are largely arbitrary, and tend often
to have a lot to do with the current time and place (e.g. specfic laws and/or
the breaking of them, current notions of what is [politically] "correct" or
"upstanding" behaviour, and so forth.) Ethics are things that most people (as
far as I have seen, and having been an anthropology student for about 6 years
now I have seen fairly far) seem to take for granted, to consider obvious.
There is a revulsion factor to the breaking of ethical taboos that has little
if any counterpart to the breaking of moral taboos, except in cases where a
good deal of fanaticism is involved on one side or another.  I would argue,
but cannot currently prove, that what I call ethics are far older than morals,
and in short are the bare minimum social regulations to get along in a culture
of any sort.  One could say that they are rooted in "The Golden Rule" (or that
ethics and TGR stem from the same source.)

To return to the quoted material, I think if one considers this (and buys it,
of course :) the question of whether random violence is [im|a]moral is not
the question to ask.  It would seem that it could certainly be unethical,
and from my researches it appears to be just that in almost all cultures, even
rather violent ones, while the more relative morality of such an act is easily
moldable to the situation.  If in doubt, I ask you to ponder the "Waco Inci-
dent" in these terms.

Anyway, I'd be quite interested in discussing this with anyone who cares to,
as I find the matter to be of great interest.  Most of the time, I see "moral"
and "ethical" used as synonyms, and somehow it seems to miss something.

I had begun work on an essay on this topic, but reached a few walls. Perhaps
a lively discussion on the matter will get me to writing again.

PS: If this has been adequately covered in some age old philosophy book that
I missed somehow all these years, please clue me in!

-- 
