Date: Mon, 3 May 93 09:40:48 EST
From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: TECH: Would `dark fiber' be vulnerable to jamming?

>Comes the day I go psychotic from information overload and decide it's all
>too much.  I pull the plug on my PC and shine a bright white light into the
>connector.
>
>*And the whole world network grinds to a halt!*

Not really.  The entire world won't really be a giant fiber.  It would be
many fibers linked together with signal boosters to keep the signal going.
(Your flashlight isn't strong enough to shine to the other side of the world.)
You will jam your local segment, but the checksums won't come out right, so
the signal will not be forwarded by the digital signal boosters to all fibers.

I would presume that any fiber carrier would have a mechanism to disconnect
any connections that appear to jam with bad data.  This is what we do on
our ethernet, which is a shared transmission.
