Date: Mon, 3 May 93 09:11:18 PDT
From: desilets@sj.ate.slb.com  (Mark Desilets)
Subject: Meta: Judgment Metzger vs. Moore

No one would ever accuse me of being the world's foremost Carol Moore fan,
but I must say that I find this judgement excessive.  

First, I find that charges of "intellectual dishonesty" are levied frequently
on this list, with no one taking undue umbrage.  It is used as an indication
that one feels their rhetorical opponent has strayed from logically pure
or consistent argumentation.   While I do not agree that this is flaming, I
can see why it might be found to be so by others and so could understand
a warning being given for this "violation".   I *do* feel that this incident
is being overblown by the fact that Ms Moore is not a "list favorite" to put
it mildly.  Nonetheless, if our code is not objectively fair to all 
participants, I'm not sure I want any part of it.

Second, I believe that issuing a warning for each of the infractions:

2) I find that Ms. Moore, in this post, was replying after a fight
   had broken out "trying to set the record straight."
   
and

3) I find that Ms. Moore, in this post, was replying after a fight
   had broken out "trying to up hold her honor."

is absurd.  First of all, that the list management would act in the behalf
of a member who has not (for whatever reasons) filed charges is a usurpation
of authority.  Who is to say when someone should be offended?  I say, unless
someone claims damage, no damage, or only damage below a reasonable threshold
can be said to have occurred.  It seems to me that once again extraordinary
effort has been made to make Ms Moore bear the punishment for offenses that
would not raise an eyebrow were they committed by most other members.


  Moreover, I don't believe that trying to "set the record straight" should be
an actionable item.  If I were to present an argument, and subsequently 
believed it to be misrepresented by a respondant, I would surely reply
with a message to the effect "you have misunderstoond my position.  My
statment of belief in preimse A does not imply a belief in proposition
B."   I do not find such statments unreasonable in debate nor in e-mail
discussion.  I had believed the injunction against "fighting" and "upholding
honor" to be intended to prevent more serious types of attack, more nearly
ad hominem in nature.

  I suppose it boils down to the issue of whether it is legitimate for the list
to treat different members differently based on some subjective criteria of
reputation.  In my opinion it is not legitimate.  If we want to provide 
software which allows each recipient to screen out a member, I am all for
it.  If the list administration becomes a bludgeon to weild against the
unpopular, then it becomes capricious and unreliable.


  It is my opinion that at most Ms Moore should receive one warning.  For
more than that to be levied seems patently unfair to me.  I anxiously
await discussion on this issue from other members.


