Date: Mon, 03 May 93 12:42 CST
From: Matthew J Miszewski <MJMISKI@macc.wisc.edu>
Subject: Ethics v. Morals

 
  I had a very similar discussion...er...argument just the other day
(unfortunately in the middle of State Street). It dealt with whether
there was any distillable core of morals|ethics|justice.  Once again
it came down to "the slaughter of the innocents" question. I thought
that this appeared a good point in the argument (appeared cross cultural).
 
  But my antagonist threw out a good remark. Basically, *all* of my assumptions
were based upon value judegements that were subjective.  Vitally, the
reason that the slaughter of a completely innocent person "appears"
to be im|a moral is because our cultures tell us that life is to be valued.
It is posssible therefore, for a culture to exist where the preservation
of life is not valued. Or one in which a higher value is placed on strength
as opposed to mere existance.
 
  I countered of course that no such community could exist, because its
basic social structure would collapse upon itself. My opponent came back
with the claim that even that justification is itself a value judgement
(that society should exist). I have been frustrated about this issue for
some time before this argument ever took place.  Im glad its taking
place here too.
 
  What it boiled down to was me trying to rationalize the existence of
Platonic Ideals because of the notable difference in degrees of injustice
(i.e. The slaughter of innocent people appears more unjust than does
patenting an algorhithm 8-).  My query was whether this *appearance* was
due to the existence of some objective justice-thing which could be
extrapolatable to from the two distinct points of injustice, or were
the differences due completely to social value systems?
 
  My friend offered the mathematical explanation that it was possible
for the "justice curve" to simply be similar to all other curves that
infinitely approach the axis but never actually touch it.  I've always
hated the existance of such curves, but cannot rationally overcome the argument.
This explanation does sort of beg the question though. I mean, couldnt
objective justice still exist outside of the graph?
 
  In answer to those asking for answers: There are none, stick to asking
questions!  8-)
 
